 "pattern": "Digit-by-digit addition with a carry",
 "statement": "Two non-negative integers are stored as linked lists with their digits in **reverse order** (ones digit first). Add them and return the sum as a linked list in the same format.",
 "example": {
  "input": "l1 = [5,6], l2 = [7,8,9]",
  "output": "[2,5,0,1]",
  "why": "The lists mean 65 and 987. 65 + 987 = 1052, which is stored as 2 → 5 → 0 → 1."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "l1 = [2,4,3], l2 = [5,6,4]",
   "output": "[7,0,8]"
  },
  {
   "input": "l1 = [0], l2 = [0]",
   "output": "[0]"
  },
  {
   "input": "l1 = [9,9,9], l2 = [1]",
   "output": "[0,0,0,1]"
  }
 ],
 "picture": "Picture adding two long numbers on paper, column by column, starting with the ones. You write down the last digit of each column's sum and carry the 1, if there is one, into the next column. Here the digits are stored ones first, which is exactly the order you add them in, so you simply walk along both lists side by side.",
 "idea": [
  "The digits come lowest first — exactly the order in which we add by hand: ones, then tens, then hundreds.",
  "Walk both lists together. At each position add the two digits and the **carry** from the previous position. Write down `total % 10` and carry `total // 10`.",
  "If one list is shorter, treat its missing digits as 0. If a carry is left at the very end, it becomes one more digit."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def addTwoNumbers(self, l1: ListNode, l2: ListNode) -> ListNode:\n        dummy = ListNode()\n        current = dummy\n        carry = 0\n\n        while l1 or l2:\n            val1 = l1.val if l1 else 0\n            val2 = l2.val if l2 else 0\n            total = val1 + val2 + carry\n\n            carry = total // 10\n            digit = total % 10\n\n            current.next = ListNode(digit)\n            current = current.next\n\n            if l1:\n                l1 = l1.next\n            if l2:\n                l2 = l2.next\n\n        if carry:\n            current.next = ListNode(carry)\n\n        return dummy.next",
 "say": {
  "1": "LeetCode puts every answer inside a class named `Solution`. It is only a wrapper: the real work happens in the function below it.",
  "2": "The function we must write. It gets two numbers stored as lists of digits (ones first) and returns their sum, stored the same way.",
  "3": "A placeholder node to hang the answer's digits on.",
  "4": "`current` is the last digit written so far.",
  "5": "What carries into the next column. Nothing at first.",
  "7": "Repeat while either number still has digits.",
  "8": "This column's digit from the first number, or 0 if it has run out.",
  "9": "The same for the second number.",
  "10": "Add the two digits and the carry.",
  "12": "The tens part carries into the next column: `total // 10` is 1 when the total is 10 or more, and 0 otherwise.",
  "13": "The ones part is this column's digit: `total % 10` is the remainder after dividing by 10.",
  "15": "Write that digit as a new node at the end of the answer.",
  "16": "The new node is now the end of the answer.",
  "18": "Does the first number have more digits?",
  "19": "Then move on to its next digit.",
  "20": "Does the second number have more digits?",
  "21": "Then move on to its next digit.",
  "23": "Is there a carry left after the last column?",
  "24": "Then it becomes one more digit at the end.",
  "26": "The answer starts just after the placeholder: return that node."
 },
